these chickens are engaged in  important work they produce eggs like  any other chickens  but with the help of their owner they're  also working wonders  transforming the desert this entire area  near the city of  chincha alta in peru is dry and barren  there's almost no rain so it's hardly  suitable for farming  and yet a green plantation is  flourishing  mandarin trees laden with fruit cover an  area of 30 square kilometers it's all  thanks to the chickens  and their resourceful owner  we've been farming in the desert for  many years people thought we were crazy  at first  obviously you only normally grow fruit  and good soil  but today we're producing record numbers  of mandarins  right here in the middle of the desert  it's the chickens that have made the  desert soil fertile  six million of them in all at first  pasco macias was only planning to sell  their eggs  but when he found himself ankle deep in  excrement  he came up with an idea  why not use it as fertilizer  he ants all the leftover chicken feed  too  the birds feed on corn but they're quite  picky  [Music]  this is what's left over we used to burn  it up or throw it away  but now we use it to produce biochar and  that's the central element in our  regenerative fertilizer  basco macias burns the organic waste in  a controlled process called pyrolysis  it's a centuries-old method used by the  indigenous peoples of the amazon  biochar is rich in nutrients an ideal  source of food for the trees  but then he adds the chicken excrement  too  along with a few other ingredients  turning it all into fertilizer pellets  some of them he uses for the mandarin  plantation  the rest he sells  this circular economy allows us to make  everything efficient  it's really nothing new it's actually  the way that nature works  the principle is simple resources are  used and reused as long as possible  saving money and avoiding waste unlike  normal methods  in the capital lima the german  development agency  giz is advising the peruvian environment  ministry  on how to encourage more businesses to  move to a circular economy  the program is financed by the german  government's international climate  initiative  [Music]  i'm very grateful to be part of this  project and for the opportunity to  support an initiative like this  it's a paradigm shift a whole different  way of thinking  so we need to provide help to ensure  this shift will be successful  so she talks to business owners who've  already made the change to learn from  their experience  like palmyra ocampo  he's a chef in lima and buys all his  vegetables from an organic farm  on the menu today fennel  the aroma of fennel is intense  delicious  he uses every part of the plant  including the bits other chefs normally  throw away  like the flowers  i'm going to take some of these flowers  they have a great aroma  palmiro has founded an initiative called  corey  which means treasure in the indigenous  quechua language  part of the idea is to train other chefs  to use food in its entirety  we founded corey with the aim of making  peruvian cuisine more sustainable  by optimizing our use of food the aim is  to avoid food waste and hunger in peru  he takes his freshly harvested  vegetables to a local soup kitchen  staff here are grateful for tips they  work as volunteers cooking for the needy  they can't afford to throw anything away  a meal here  costs one euro but for those without an  income  it's available for free  [Music]  ronald vargaman shows the cooking team a  new recipe  a fennel and beetroot salad that uses  every part of the vegetable  it's served with a vinaigrette made of  fruit peel and a kind of peppercorn  made of dried papaya seeds look at this  papaya we can spoon out the seeds  wonderfully  without losing anything  normally the seeds and peel would land  in the trash  here they end up in the sauce  the fennel flowers provide the finishing  touch  i'm happy and grateful we were throwing  a lot away before  now we use it and don't waste anything  the chefs are now showing others how  it's done bosco macias also wants to  share his idea for fertilizer  and see this form of circular economy  take off elsewhere  if fruit can flourish in the desert the  possibilities  are surely endless  you
 

